'''Asiatic''' (ā-shi- or ā-zhi-at′ik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< L. ''Asiaticus'', < Gr. Ἀσιατικός, < Ἀσία, L. ''Asia'', Asia: see ''Asian''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Belonging to or characteristic of Asia or its inhabitants.—'''2.''' Characterized by Asianism.—{{smaller|'''Asiatic cholera.''' See ''cholera''.—'''Asiatic pills,''' in ''med.'', pills of arsenious oxid and black pepper.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A native of Asia.

'''Asiaticism''' (ā-shi- or ā-zhi-at′i-sizm), ''n.'' [< ''Asiatic'' + ''-ism''.] Something characteristic of Asiatics; specifically, Asiatic, as distinguished from European, modes of thought and life. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The great struggle between Protestantism and ''Asiaticism.''
{{right|''New Eng. Jour. of Education'', XX. 75.}}}}

'''Asiaticization''' (ā-shi- or ā-zhi-at″i-si-zā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ''Asiaticize'' + ''-ation''.] The act of rendering Asiatic, or of permeating with Asiaticism. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The ''Asiaticization'' of European life.
{{right|''J. Fiske'', Amer. Pol. Ideas, p. 117.}}}}

'''Asiaticize''' (ā-shi- or ā-zhi-at′i-sīz), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''Asiaticized'', ppr. ''Asiaticizing''. [< ''Asiatic'' + ''-ize''.] To render Asiatic; tinge or imbue with Asiatic ideas, customs, etc. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|The close of the seventeenth century, which marks the culmination of the ''Asiaticizing'' tendency in Europe, saw despotism, both political and religious, firmly established in France, and Spain, and Italy, and in half of Germany.
{{right|''J. Fiske'', Amer. Pol. Ideas, p. 119.}}}}

'''Asida''' (as′i-dä), ''n.'' [NL.] The typical genus of beetles of the subfamily ''Asidinæ'', containing numerous wingless species with ovate bodies, inhabiting desert regions of Europe and North America.

